From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Benjamin Gregoire <Benjamin.Gregoire@inria.fr>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 15:37:08 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] sem_pexpr takes the mode; the initialisation tests observe
 the raw state

The sections SEM_PEXPR and SEM_EASSERT of psem_defs.v take a SemMode in
context: the variable reads (through get_gvar, which takes the mode), the
array and memory accesses and the operators of sem_pexpr and sem_eassert,
and the accesses of write_lval (Lasub included) follow the same instance,
and sem_pexpr (sm := total) is the total semantics. get_var_is, which reads
the results of a call, stays in the partial mode. Without an instance in
context the default one, partial, keeps every existing statement.

Two tests of sem_eassert must not follow the mode, since the total mode
never reads an uninitialised variable or byte: Pis_var_init uses
Vm.is_var_init, the raw entry of the varmap, and Pis_mem_init uses validr,
defined on the partial read. PappN_safety is unchanged: WArray.is_init
reads the array data, which the two modes update alike.

One proof adapted, eq_on_sem_eassert, through Vm.is_var_initE. The OCaml
evaluator passes the partial mode to sem_pexpr(s), write_lval(s) and
sem_assert.

(cherry picked from commit fe6f7aa58690aff2412c6a1433a3e033da14a8a1)
